# Exercise: Real Echo Server
#
# This is the capstone. By the end of this exercise you have written
# a working TCP echo round-trip — server and client cooperating in
# the same program on the same machine.
#
# Two roles, one program:
#
#   echo-server  — runs in a SPAWNED strand. Plays the SERVER role:
#                  accept, read, echo, close. (Already written below;
#                  study it as the canonical shape you'll write again.)
#   test-echo    — runs on the MAIN strand. After spawning the
#                  server, plays the CLIENT role: connect, write,
#                  read, assert, close.
#
# Both ends run cooperatively under the same scheduler. Every
# `net.tcp.*` parking point gives the scheduler a chance to run
# the other strand. Data dependencies enforce ordering — accept
# can't return until main connects; main's read can't return
# until echo-server's write completes.
#
# This is a one-shot echo server: it accepts one connection and
# returns. A multi-connection server would loop on accept; we
# don't loop here because the test asserts exactly one round-trip.
#
# Socket ownership:
#
#   - Main owns the listener. It calls listen and closes it after
#     the assertion. The spawned strand only sees a *copy* of the
#     listener via copy-on-spawn — it uses that copy to accept,
#     then drops it without closing.
#   - The accepted socket is owned by the spawned strand; the
#     connect socket is owned by main. Each closes its own end.
#
# Your task: complete the test-echo body — the CLIENT side.
# Connect, write "ping", read, assert "ping", close.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

# ───────── SERVER (runs in the spawned strand) ─────────
# seq:allow(unchecked-tcp-write)
: echo-server ( Socket -- )
    dup net.tcp.accept
    [ # accept ok:  ( listener accepted )
      dup net.tcp.read
      [ # read ok:  ( listener accepted data )
        over net.tcp.write drop
      ]
      [ drop ]
      if
      net.tcp.close drop      # close the accepted socket (we own it)
      drop                     # drop our copy of the listener (we don't)
    ]
    [ # accept failed
      drop drop
    ]
    if
;

# ───────── MAIN: spawns the server, then plays the CLIENT ─────────
# seq:allow(unchecked-tcp-write)
: test-echo ( -- )
    18265 net.tcp.listen
    [ # listen ok:  ( listener )
      [ echo-server ] strand.spawn drop         # ── server strand queued ──

      # --- below this line, main plays the CLIENT ---
      # Your code here: open the client side of the echo.
      # Stack when you start:  ( listener )
      # Stack when you finish: ( listener )   ← so the close below fires
      #
      # Steps:
      #   1. net.tcp.connect to "127.0.0.1" 18265
      #   2. on connect success: write "ping", read, assert the
      #      response is "ping", close the connect socket. Use
      #      a `[ ok ] [ err ] if` on the read Bool.
      #   3. on connect failure: drop the junk socket and fail the test.
      false test.assert                # placeholder

      # Do not edit below this line
      net.tcp.close drop               # main closes the listener
    ]
    [ drop ]
    if
;
